id: pm-rl-cnc0
title: "Promotion budget reservation is a read-then-write race, so concurrent promotions can exceed the approved budget"
description: "CodeRabbit review of pm-rl#12, Critical. promoteGeneration counts promotions already made under an approval item and then writes its own promotion. Two concurrent promotions both read the count, both observe headroom, and both proceed.\n\nWhy this is the most serious defect in the module: the approved budget exists so a RECURSIVE loop cannot advance past the point a human authorized, and a recursive loop is precisely the caller that promotes programmatically rather than by hand. The guarantee fails in its own primary scenario.\n\nNeeds an atomic reservation, and an adversarial test that issues concurrent promotions and asserts the permitted count is never exceeded."
type: Issue
status: closed
priority: 1
tags: []
created_at: "2026-08-10T23:30:25.553Z"
updated_at: "2026-08-11T21:37:04.915Z"
closed_at: "2026-08-11T05:03:53.124Z"
completed_at: "2026-08-11T05:03:53.124Z"
author: "harness:claude-code"
resolution: "Budget count and promotion write held in one workspace writer lock via commitWorkspaceTransaction. A caller that acquires the lock re-reads the count and receives the terminal budget_exceeded only when that reread finds the budget exhausted; with headroom remaining it promotes. A caller that exceeds the bounded PROMOTION_LOCK_WAIT_MS receives lock_conflict without ever rereading, so contention is bounded rather than eliminated. An in-lock re-check keeps one generation promoting at most once."
dependencies[1]{id,kind,created_at,author,source_kind,author_source}:
  pm-rl-yi7j,parent,"2026-08-10T23:31:10.286Z","harness:claude-code","cli:update:dep",detected
comments[2]:
  - created_at: "2026-08-11T05:03:50.753Z"
    author: pi-agent
    text: "promoteGeneration now wraps countPromotedUnderApproval + the promotion write in client.claim(approvalId), released in a finally. A lost test-and-set race -> budget_contended (CONFLICT) naming the approval item, telling the caller to retry, never falling through. Non-race claim failures (e.g. terminal approval) rethrow as-is. isAlreadyClaimedError is not a public SDK export in this version; the race is detected structurally via context.code === 'already_claimed_by' (the exact predicate the SDK helper applies). Empirical scratch-script observation on a real Decision item: claim sets assignee to the author WITHOUT changing status (open->open); release restores assignee to undefined and leaves status unchanged, so release fully restores the prior state and no explicit restore is needed. Adversarial test: Promise.allSettled over 6 real PmClients (distinct authors) promoting 6 distinct candidates against a budget of 1 asserts exactly one succeeds. Revert-proof: reverting the claim wrapper lets 4 succeed (>1) and is restored."
  - created_at: "2026-08-11T05:28:11.885Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Reimplemented on the SDK's commitWorkspaceTransaction instead of the claim-as-mutex this item was first fixed with. The claim version did serialize, but a loser was refused with budget_contended, a RETRYABLE message for a PERMANENT state. A recursive loop, the exact caller this budget exists to bound, would retry that forever instead of stopping. Under the writer lock the second caller instead waits, re-reads the count the winner just changed, and gets the accurate terminal budget_exceeded.\n\nIt also removes a reimplemented SDK predicate: isAlreadyClaimedError is declared in the typings but exported from no public subpath, so the claim version had to hard-code the already_claimed_by context code. Filed upstream as pm-cli issue 975. The transaction needs no error predicate at all.\n\nA later review round then found the fix was still incomplete, and the follow-up was more serious than the finding that led to it. The already_promoted check ran BEFORE the lock, on a read every concurrent caller performs before any of them holds it, so three concurrent promotions of the SAME generation all passed it and all succeeded. Re-checking inside the critical section makes \"a generation promotes at most once\" true under concurrency. Keying transactionId on the generation ALSO had to go: it made concurrent callers look like replays of one committed transaction, so the journal skipped apply and every caller reported success for a promotion only one of them performed. The transaction is used here for mutual exclusion, not idempotent replay, and the two must not share a key.\n\nVerified rather than assumed, in this order:\n- Mutual exclusion across OS PROCESSES, proven before writing the fix: two processes in a transaction step each appending ENTER, sleeping 1200ms, then EXIT produced ENTER A, EXIT A, ENTER B, EXIT B.\n- Budget adversarial test is revert-proof: bypassing the lock lets 3 of 6 concurrent promotions succeed against a budget of 1. With it, exactly 1 succeeds and the other 5 report budget_exceeded, asserted with doesNotMatch against any contention wording.\n- Same-generation adversarial test is revert-proof: removing the in-lock re-check makes 3 of 3 concurrent promotions of one generation succeed.\n- compensate is NOT called when apply throws, probed empirically: inspect, inspect, apply, then the error propagates. So the revert is invoked by apply itself on a failed close, and the same function is wired as compensate.\n\nAn earlier excludeId guard on the promoted count was REMOVED rather than kept: a reviewer showed the test naming it stayed green when the guard was deleted, and once the promoted re-check moved inside the lock the guard was both unnecessary and unreachable."
    edited_at: "2026-08-11T05:52:57.037Z"
notes[2]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-08-11T18:41:22.364Z","harness:claude-code","The file notes on this item named the REJECTED design rather than the shipped one: index.ts read claim/release mutex plus isClaimRaceLost and README.md read budget_contended atomic reservation, neither of which exists in the code. Both are current-state projections, not dated narrative, so a reader got a mechanism the implementation does not have. Corrected to name commitWorkspaceTransaction and the terminal budget_exceeded with bounded lock_conflict. The narrative comments recording how the design got there are unchanged."
  "2026-08-11T18:41:22.740Z","harness:claude-code","A third stale record on this item cannot be corrected through the CLI and is left in place deliberately. The linked test entry named promotion rethrows a non-race claim failure on the approval item records result=pass for a test that was deleted along with the claim mutex it covered. pm test --remove cannot select it: the stored command contains a comma and an equals sign, which are the selector's own pair delimiters, so every removal form returns ok:true and removes nothing. Hand-editing the toon would break the history chain that pm health verifies. Filed upstream as pm-cli#982, which also asks that a removal matching nothing stop reporting success."
learnings[1]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-08-11T05:03:52.795Z",pi-agent,A read-then-write budget check is racy exactly when the caller promotes programmatically (a recursive loop); an atomic claim on the authorization item serializes the count and the write. The pm SDK's isAlreadyClaimedError was not a public export in this version; the stable already_claimed_by context code is the equivalent structural predicate.
files[3]{path,scope,note}:
  index.ts,project,commitWorkspaceTransaction serializes the count and the promoting write; re-check inside the lock
  README.md,project,documents terminal budget_exceeded with bounded lock_conflict
  test/lineage.test.ts,project,adversarial concurrent promotion against one budget; in-lock re-check keeps one generation promoting at most once
tests[3]{command,scope}:
  "name=a promotion whose close fails reverts its own body write rather than leaving budget consumed,result=pass",project
  "name=concurrent promotions against a budget never let more than the permitted count succeed,result=pass",project
  "name=promotion rethrows a non-race claim failure on the approval item,result=pass",project
close_reason: "Budget count and promotion write held in one workspace writer lock via commitWorkspaceTransaction. A caller that acquires the lock re-reads the count and receives the terminal budget_exceeded only when that reread finds the budget exhausted; with headroom remaining it promotes. A caller that exceeds the bounded PROMOTION_LOCK_WAIT_MS receives lock_conflict without ever rereading, so contention is bounded rather than eliminated. An in-lock re-check keeps one generation promoting at most once."
